# What is Computational Thinking


Computational thinking is a problem-solving approach that involves breaking problems down into smaller parts, recognizing patterns, focusing on important details, and creating step-by-step solutions that a computer or person can follow.
 

<h2 style="text-align: center;">Pillars of Computational Thinking</h2>

<img src="https://images.code.org/c83c7a9e5a895ad1ec7aab29c841717d-image-1743009092468.png" alt="Pillars with icons for computational thinking: decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction, algorithm design." width="600px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">


---

In **Foundations of AI Programming**, students explore computational thinking skills before diving into the programing lessons. 


<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #222222; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px 20px; width: 90%; margin: 20px auto;">
<p><b>💡 Teacher tip:</b> Computational thinking gives your students the problem-solving habits they need before they ever write a line of Python. When they can organize ideas, spot patterns, and lay out clear logical steps, they walk into the programming lessons ready to think like programmers, not just type code.</p>
</div>


